<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahura Mazda</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ormazd, Horomazes, Auramazdā, Ōhramazdē</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahura Mazda, also known as Ormazd and Horomazes, is the principal God and sky deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and the spirit most often invoked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasna">Yasna</a>; the words Ahura and Mazda mean lord and wisdom. Some historians hold that his worship existed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a>, who according to Zoroastrian tradition was led into his presence in a vision at the age of 30. His first notable invocation is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, and until Artaxerxes II he was invoked alone in all extant royal inscriptions, then in a triad with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithra">Mithra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>. Images of him from the 5th century BCE were eventually removed under an iconoclastic movement supported by the Sasanian dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda.jpg" alt="Ahura Mazda" width="330" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derfash Kaviani (درفش کاویانی) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naqshe_Rostam_Darafsh_Ordibehesht_93_(35).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Ahura is most likely from Proto-Indo-European roots meaning to engender, and is cognate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> ásura. Mazda reflects a Proto-Iranian word meaning intelligence or wisdom. Some scholars connect Ahura Mazda with the Vedic Varuna. Some Zoroastrian historians believe his worship existed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a>; R. C. Zaehner thought the earlier Ahura Mazda was associated with truth or universal order, and with water, light or the sun.</p><p>According to Zoroastrian tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> received a revelation at the age of 30: he saw Vohu Manah, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amesha-spenta">Amesha Spenta</a>, who led him into the presence of Ahura Mazda and taught him the principles of the Good Religion. Zoroaster taught that the destructive spirit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahriman">Angra Mainyu</a> was Ahura Mazda's inferior, not his equal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Whether the Achaemenid kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> is much debated, but they worshipped Ahura Mazda, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a> refers to him many times. In the early Achaemenid period he had no representation; from Cyrus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> an empty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> drawn by white horses was kept for him to accompany the army into battle. A winged figure once read as Ahura Mazda is now thought to represent the royal khvarenah, divine power and glory. Images began in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a> in the late 5th century BCE.</p><p>Zoroastrian iconoclasm, traceable from the end of the Parthian period and the start of the Sasanian, ended images of him in worship, though he remained symbolised by a dignified male figure in investiture scenes. In the Sasanian period a divergent form, Zurvanism, made Ahura Mazda and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahriman">Angra Mainyu</a> twin sons of Zurvan. Five Sasanian kings took the name Hormizd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> gives a Greek account of Oromazes, born from the purest light, at war with Areimanius, born from darkness; the scholar Mary Boyce judged it a fairly accurate knowledge of the basics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> the name Ohrmazd Bay was used for the primal figure, the original man. In Sogdian Buddhism the name appears as Xwrmztʼ, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> it reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> as Qormusta Tengri. The pre-Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> held Aramazd as an important deity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahura Mazda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion before Christianity was a pantheon headed by Aramazd, cognate of the Iranian <b>Ahura Mazda</b> and identified with Zeus, with Anahit, goddess of fertility and birth, whose temples stood at Armavir, Artashat and Ashtishat; Mihr, god of the sun and light, at Bagaharich and Garni; Vahagn the storm…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Gathas, he received a revelation from <b>Ahura Mazda</b>, the “Wise Lord”, whom he proclaimed as the supreme creator, uncreated and wholly good.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sogdians spoke an Eastern Iranian language related to Bactrian and Khwarazmian, and the Avesta, the scripture of Zoroastrianism, names the land of the Sogdians second among the good lands created by <b>Ahura Mazda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vendidad, a name that derives from words meaning given against the demons, has 22 sections called fragards, framed as discussions between <b>Ahura Mazda</b> and Zoroaster, and it deals with laws rather than recording a ceremony.</blockquote><a href="/work/avesta">Avesta · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Darius presents himself as victorious in every battle and credits his success to the grace of <b>Ahura Mazda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>A widely circulated translation that credits the Cylinder with abolishing slavery and guaranteeing a minimum wage and asylum is false: it makes claims that are not on the real cylinder and names the Zoroastrian god <b>Ahura Mazda</b> instead of Marduk.</blockquote><a href="/work/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
